Keep SELF as SELF in the shared value (P-285)

94.8% of permission_entry was one SID. Measured on a real forest before this
change: 34,593 of 36,484 rows were S-1-5-10. After it, 38.

ContentKey serialised the whole PermissionEntry including Trustee.Name, and
ObjectPermissions resolves PRINCIPAL SELF to "This object (Alice)" against the
object being read. So Alice's SELF entry and Bob's hashed differently and never
shared a row. That is exactly what kept P-279 correct - sharing the row would
have shown one object's name against another object's permission - and it is why
the table P-279 built to deduplicate barely deduplicated on a real directory.
The synthetic bench could not say so, because it holds no SELF entries at all: it
reported a 712:1 collapse and a clean success.

THE RAW ACCESS CONTROL ENTRY IS EVIDENCE AND THE NAME IS PRESENTATION. What the
directory holds is the SID. "This object (Alice)" is this product saying what
that means when read against Alice. PermissionTrustee now owns both forms -
Shared() and DisplayedAgainst(dn) - and WellKnownTrustee builds the displayed
form through the same method, so the sentence is spelled in exactly one place
rather than once in capture and once in the judgement's read path.

SharedPermissionStorage generalises before hashing and resolves after verifying.
The occurrence fingerprint therefore covers the target, the generation and the
raw entry rather than the raw entry and a rendering of the target - the same
binding it always was, with presentation taken out of it. The order is
load-bearing and is enforced by the fingerprint itself: resolving before the
check makes every SELF proof fail, which a mutation confirmed.

JudgementSchema.Version is 44. A version 43 store's occurrence fingerprints were
taken over the displayed form and do not verify against this build, so it is
refused on version rather than reported as tampered proofs, which is what the
number is for. The layout checkpoint carries 43's DDL hash because the physical
layout is unchanged - permission_entry.name already existed and only its content
differs - the same way 33 through 38 repeated theirs.

MEASURED, on ad.lab-20260923-231917, same evidence and same seal, against a
baseline recorded before the change:

  permission_entry                 36,484 -> 1,929   (-94.7%)
    of which S-1-5-10              34,593 ->    38   (-99.9%)
    of which every other trustee    1,891 -> 1,891   unchanged
  permission_classification_entry 209,252 -> 209,252 UNCHANGED
  judgement on disk                107.9 MB -> 95.5 MB
  rules ran / findings             17 / 27 -> 17 / 27
  judging wall clock               22.8 s -> 23.2 s

The occurrence count not moving is the check that mattered. Each of those 209,252
rows is the distinct fact that this object, at this position, carries that entry,
and an audit tool that lost one would be failing to report a permission the
directory holds. What changed is how many times the entry behind them is written
down. Rules and findings are identical, so no verdict moved as a side effect of
changing how a trustee is stored. The prediction recorded in the baseline before
the change was "roughly 1,900".

The store fell 11.5% and not 94.7% because permission_entry was never the large
table. permission_classification_entry is, at 209,252 rows, and shrinking that is
P-280. What this removes is the reason P-279's saving did not survive contact with
a real forest, and it removes it before P-280 chooses a sharing unit against a
table that was 95% one SID.

THREE CHECKS, and the third is the negative control: two objects carrying the
identical SELF entry share one row, each still reads back naming itself rather
than the other, and the stored row carries the catalogue's name rather than either
object's. Without the third, a build that stored one object's rendering and
re-derived nothing would satisfy the first two. Four mutations with explicit
rebuilds either side: not generalising killed two checks, not re-resolving on read
killed the misattribution check the review caught, resolving before verifying
killed only the SELF check - correct, since DisplayedAgainst is a no-op for every
other trustee - and generalising every trustee rather than only SELF killed three
including the field-distinctness theory.

One existing test changed rather than being worked around.
FullControlRoundTripsEverySourceFieldIncludingUnsupportedInputs named S-1-5-10 to
exercise odd field values; the trustee's name is no longer a round-tripped source
field for that SID, so it now names an ordinary one and says why. The SELF
behaviour is checked directly by the three new tests rather than as a side effect
there.

On the production capture the same defect covers 20,454,613 occurrences, measured
in next/artifacts/p280-shape-profile/.
